{
  "scenario": {
    "id": "P13",
    "title": "Boot fault - startup code forgets to copy .data from flash",
    "layer": "2 Boot",
    "description": "The same program as P01, but the reset handler skips the .data copy loop. The initial value of the global \"greeting_len\" (7) sits in flash at __data_load, but nothing copies it to its run-time address in SRAM, so the program reads whatever SRAM held at power-on. .bss is still zeroed, so the uninitialised counter is correct. Nothing faults; one number is simply wrong. Compare with P02, the mirror-image bug.\n",
    "program": "blink",
    "options": {},
    "faults": [
      {
        "type": "missing_data_copy"
      }
    ],
    "config": {
      "seed": 1,
      "max_cycles": 50000
    },
    "expected": {
      "summary": "Boot reaches main without faults; the data_copied step reports a mismatch; \"counter=1\" is correct but \"greeting_len=\" is not 7.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "check": "boot_step",
          "step": "data_copied",
          "ok": false
        },
        {
          "check": "boot_step",
          "step": "bss_zeroed",
          "ok": true
        },
        {
          "check": "no_faults"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_contains",
          "text": "counter=1"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_not_contains",
          "text": "greeting_len=7"
        },
        {
          "check": "global_ne",
          "name": "greeting_len",
          "value": 7
        }
      ]
    },
    "what_to_look_for": "The data_copied boot step says the check FAILED and prints the mismatch, for example \"greeting_len: SRAM=0x5D6E... expected 0x00000007\". The bss_zeroed step passes. The UART shows \"boot ok\" and \"counter=1\" (from .bss, correct) but \"greeting_len=\" followed by a large random number. image_map shows the two addresses involved: data_load (the flash copy of the initial values, 0x100001xx) and data.greeting_len (0x20000000, its run-time home). Run \"r2s disasm blink\" to see both in the map and the copy loop that is missing here.\n",
    "likely_root_causes": [
      "The reset handler has no .data copy loop, or it copies from the wrong source symbol (the run-time address instead of the load address).",
      "The linker script does not give .data separate load (AT> FLASH) and run (> RAM) addresses, so __data_load is wrong.",
      "The loop bounds use __data_start/__data_end from a different section, or are not word aligned, so the last globals are skipped.",
      "The variable is const and was expected in flash, but a cast or a missing const put it in .data."
    ],
    "how_to_debug": [
      "Before main runs, compare the two copies in GDB: 'x/4wx &__data_load' (flash) and 'x/4wx &__data_start' (SRAM). They must be equal after the copy loop.",
      "'arm-none-eabi-objdump -h' shows .data with different VMA (0x2000xxxx) and LMA (0x100xxxxx); if they are equal, the linker script is missing AT> FLASH.",
      "Set a watchpoint on the global ('watch greeting_len') and reset: if it never changes before main, the copy never ran.",
      "Power-cycle the board: a value that changes between power cycles is power-on SRAM garbage, not a logic bug."
    ],
    "what_this_means": "An initialised global has two addresses: where its initial value is stored (flash, the load address) and where the program uses it (SRAM, the run address). Hardware never copies one to the other; the startup code does. P02 and P13 are the two halves of the same lesson: C's promises about globals are kept by the code that runs before main.\n",
    "hidden_symptom": "'boot ok' and 'counter=1' print correctly, but a configuration value that was initialised in the source reads as a random number."
  },
